WEBVTT

1
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.480
Filming is under way for the movie "Death Camp in Karasjok,"

2
00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:25.840
which tells about Serbs who died during World War II in Norway.

3
00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:28.340
Part of the film is also being shot in Niš.

4
00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:32.420
With us is producer Branko Dimeski, good day and welcome,

5
00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:38.740
and the film's director from Norway, Kjetil Palmquist, good day and welcome to "Južne vesti."

6
00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:40.680
Thank you, glad to be with you.

7
00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:46.960
To begin with I'll ask: you have been in Norway since 2008,

8
00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:51.080
how did you come up with the idea of making this film about the suffering of Serbs?

9
00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:59.300
After all, as we've heard, more than 4000 people from the territory of the former Yugoslavia,

10
00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:04.820
most of them from Serbia, were taken to Norway during World War II,

11
00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:06.900
and more than half did not return home.

12
00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:14.660
Right. In 2008, when I left and since I've been living in Norway,

13
00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:17.300
I got to know many Norwegian friends

14
00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:23.500
and saw that there are many people who know history well,

15
00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.900
especially regarding the suffering of Serbian prisoners in Norwegian camps,

16
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:29.400
and that there is a strong bond of friendship.

17
00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:37.140
That is what prompted me to think that this story might be worth putting on screen.

18
00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:42.640
14–15 years ago I met Mr. Kjetil Palmquist,

19
00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:47.280
who to date has made many films on this topic.

20
00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:56.400
And we began planning how we might also adapt for the screen the story of the suffering of Serbian prisoners in Norwegian camps.

21
00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:02.320
Then we naturally got in touch with the historian and writer Knut Flovik Thoresen,

22
00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:05.540
who to date has written about 15 books,

23
00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:10.100
and also wrote a book on precisely this subject,

24
00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:12.520
which has been translated into our language too.

25
00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:19.180
That's how this project started,

26
00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:24.120
later we got in touch with some of our historians in Serbia,

27
00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:29.080
I want to mention Mr. Đorđe Bojanić, a historian from Niš,

28
00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:35.260
who at any moment met us halfway and provided a huge amount of information on this subject.

29
00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:40.060
Some of the stories you film in Serbia, some in Norway, so here's a question for your colleague:

30
00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:48.360
how do Norwegians react when they're told about crimes committed in their country?

31
00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:59.480
The Serbian prisoners, that is, inmates — their camps were in the far north of Norway,

32
00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:05.240
and the most people who remember these events live precisely in those parts, in the far north of Norway.

33
00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:14.520
And, of course, they still remember it to this day and don't forget when it comes to the suffering of Serbian prisoners in Norwegian camps.

34
00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:27.800
How hard is it for them to talk about crimes committed in their country?

35
00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:38.280
The interviews we're conducting for this film with people who are direct eyewitnesses of this suffering,

36
00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:45.900
which has not been told until now, are very painful and difficult, and they tell them very emotionally,

37
00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:50.080
but our task is to save this from oblivion.

38
00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:53.700
What were the camps in Norway like?

39
00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:58.400
You know, I happened to visit Karasjok,

40
00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:05.720
and the stories I heard, and what I personally experienced, are very...

41
00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:09.880
well, it's very hard for me to talk about it,

42
00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:14.620
because you really feel the groaning of these people,

43
00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:22.200
especially when you hear people's testimonies, your blood runs cold, it's very hard to bear.

44
00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:27.400
I simply couldn't believe I was 4–5 thousand km from my country

45
00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:30.200
and found there the suffering of my people,

46
00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:37.720
and I asked myself whether there is a place on this planet where the Serbian people did not suffer.

47
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:46.320
Did you know, before you went to live there, how many people from Serbia died there during World War II?

48
00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:52.180
I knew, of course, but not to such an extent, not to the extent that I learned later,

49
00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:59.620
when I went to live in Norway, got to know friends and began to research —

50
00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:03.360
then I received more information on this subject.

51
00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:12.140
You've read many historical documents, got access to them. Which stories shook you the most?

52
00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:20.420
Right. Taking this opportunity, I'd thank the historian Knut Flovik Thoresen,

53
00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:27.300
who, I can confidently say, is the most authoritative on this topic

54
00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:31.380
and who also helped me learn some things,

55
00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:39.020
and I saw that more than 95% of those who died in the camps in Norway were actually Serbs.

56
00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:52.880
That's why we consider it our duty to pass this story on through this film to the younger generations in Serbia as well,

57
00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:56.660
so they don't forget the suffering of their ancestors.

58
00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:05.900
How did you take these testimonies and conversations — both with your fellow citizens and with people from Serbia?

59
00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:12.240
It's very emotional and very hard.

60
00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:20.660
It's very painful for all of us who are on the film set and listening to this,

61
00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:26.640
so he can confidently say: listening to all this is very emotional and painful.

62
00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:29.020
And it leaves a mark on him.

63
00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:35.020
As for locations in Serbia: you're filming in Niš, filming in a few other places too.

64
00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:38.280
Which places here in the south will you visit?

65
00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:42.800
Right. This is our second time in Niš.

66
00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:47.980
I'd like to thank all the good people of the city of Niš, the media, you and everyone else.

67
00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:55.880
And also the Palilula city municipality. Today for the second time we'll be filming the "Red Cross" camp.

68
00:06:55.880 --> 00:07:00.920
From here many prisoners were actually sent to death, to Norway.

69
00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:04.860
They were young men, around 30, right?

70
00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:17.040
You know, there is data that children from age 16 were sent, there were a few cases. So it's terrible.

71
00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:20.620
How many survivors are left? Mostly descendants?

72
00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:25.740
How many people have you managed to find who tell about all this?

73
00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:37.820
Many descendants have responded, which is surprisingly good, and we welcome all these good people.

74
00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:46.220
Unfortunately there are no survivors, but many descendants have responded who want to bear witness to the suffering of their ancestors.

75
00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:49.080
What do you want to achieve with this film?

76
00:07:52.520 --> 00:08:01.020
It's very important that historical events are not forgotten and are passed on to new generations, so that this never happens again.

77
00:08:01.160 --> 00:08:03.080
And how hard is it to work on such stories?

78
00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:11.560
No matter where we work and film, in Serbia or in Norway,

79
00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:14.760
many times we found ourselves in a situation where we had to interrupt filming,

80
00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:20.500
because the intensity of emotion was so great that we cried along with the witnesses.

81
00:08:20.980 --> 00:08:24.820
Have you made films about war crimes and suffering before?

82
00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:50.320
He has been working for many years on the theme of history and the events of World War II

83
00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:54.380
and to date has interviewed more than a hundred people,

84
00:08:54.380 --> 00:09:00.000
it's very hard and requires a lot of time and labor.

85
00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:06.160
You've filmed a lot, heard a lot. What did these camps in Norway look like?

86
00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:14.800
The camps in Karasjok were open-type. The temperature — minus 50 degrees.

87
00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:23.340
People almost without clothes. There was cruel torture by the guards,

88
00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:32.580
and those who couldn't cope with the assigned tasks, building the road, were killed.

89
00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:38.620
I happened to visit the camps, of course, the remains of the camps,

90
00:09:38.620 --> 00:09:43.620
and, believe me, there's no word with which a person could describe it.

91
00:09:44.020 --> 00:09:45.820
What did you feel when you entered the camp?

92
00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:50.600
When I got there, the first thing I felt — was that I feel nothing.

93
00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:55.620
I simply, really, believe me, I cried.

94
00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:04.860
With me was a Norwegian who was also there, an old man, and we cried together.

95
00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:12.220
How do you think the audience in Serbia will react to this film, and how will the audience in Norway?

96
00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:18.800
He hopes this film will be accepted,

97
00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:23.820
especially since younger generations don't have such an idea of the historical details,

98
00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:34.020
and that this film will help us remember what the suffering of our ancestors in history was like.

99
00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:40.800
What interesting data, important data, concerning the Red Cross camp did you find?

100
00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:45.600
Do you have data on how many prisoners were taken from here and how many maybe came back?

101
00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:51.040
You know, as for the "Red Cross" camp, we don't have an exact number,

102
00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:55.040
but we know that very many were taken to Norway.

103
00:10:55.040 --> 00:10:59.980
I would single out the gentleman, that is, the prisoner Velimir Pavlović,

104
00:10:59.980 --> 00:11:06.700
one of the few who survived the camp in Karasjok, he's from these parts.

105
00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:15.580
He passed away a couple of years ago, yesterday we visited his family, filmed his daughter

106
00:11:15.580 --> 00:11:21.160
and laid a wreath of gratitude on his grave.

107
00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:27.360
When is the film planned to be finished and when will the public be able to see it?

108
00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:33.100
The plan is to finish the film in the summer of next year,

109
00:11:33.100 --> 00:11:38.140
because in June the 80th anniversary of the suffering of Serbian prisoners in Norwegian camps is marked.

110
00:11:38.720 --> 00:11:45.360
And one question for you as a producer, you're making a film about grave war crimes:

111
00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:50.440
do you think that both people and the public need so many years to pass

112
00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:59.660
for the country where the dead came from and the country where the crimes were committed to unite and make such a film?

113
00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:05.660
After all, in recent years we've seen: when it comes to films about crimes,

114
00:12:05.660 --> 00:12:13.180
about wars that took place 20 years ago on the territory of the former Yugoslavia,

115
00:12:13.180 --> 00:12:17.760
it seems that neither the public nor the states are ready to cooperate on such films.

116
00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:25.440
It's very important that such events and crimes that took place are not consigned to oblivion.

117
00:12:25.900 --> 00:12:32.440
There is great willingness — both in Norway and in Serbia — to help in a good direction,

118
00:12:32.440 --> 00:12:41.000
so that the new generations coming up see what sacrifices our ancestors made,

119
00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:46.880
so that this becomes a guidepost: such crimes must never happen again.

120
00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:50.240
The support is great in both Norway and Serbia.

121
00:12:50.240 --> 00:12:55.600
Taking this opportunity, I want to thank, also on behalf of our studio,

122
00:12:55.600 --> 00:13:02.700
the Ministry of Defence of the Kingdom of Norway, which helped in making the film,

123
00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:10.740
the Embassy of the Republic of Serbia in Norway, the church community of Saint Basil of Ostrog in Oslo,

124
00:13:10.880 --> 00:13:21.260
the Union of Serbian Associations, the Palilula city municipality (Niš), you, the media, and Đorđe Bojanić,

125
00:13:21.340 --> 00:13:24.080
editor-in-chief of the website "Serbian History."

126
00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:28.060
Thank you for giving an interview to "Južne vesti."

127
00:13:28.060 --> 00:13:28.780
Thank you too.

128
00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:34.480
You watched "15 Minutes." My name is Milica Milojević-Kostić.